{"toolCallId":"call_40f3912476b54286b3cd7c47c0c49750","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/222-deepseek-baseline/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"A quiet output mode for recommend-level.sh: one global, one parser case, one output function and one dispatch branch, with the level line shared by both human-readable paths so they cannot drift. JSON keeps precedence and default output stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2-compatible shell script |\n| **Framework** | None; `recommend-level.sh` is a standalone CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | vitest CLI project, driving the script through `spawnSync` |\n\n### Overview\nAdd a quiet output mode to `recommend-level.sh`. The change is a `QUIET_OUTPUT` global beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, one `--quiet` case in the existing argument loop, an `output_quiet()` function that prints the level line the full report starts with, and a dispatch branch that prefers JSON, then quiet, then text. Default output and every exit code stay exactly as they are.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-file CLI with one function per concern; the output format is a strategy selected once, at the end of the run.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument parser**: the `while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]` loop maps flags to globals and rejects unknown options.\n- **Scoring functions**: `calculate_loc_score`, `calculate_files_score`, `calculate_risk_score`, `calculate_complexity_score`, `determine_level`, `determine_phasing`, `calculate_confidence`. All unchanged.\n- **`output_text()`**: the current human-readable report; unchanged in content.\n- **`output_quiet()`**: new; prints the recommended-level line and nothing else.\n- **Output dispatch**: the final branch that currently chooses `output_json` or `output_text`; it gains quiet as a third option.\n- **`show_help()`**: the usage text that documents every option.\n\n### Data Flow\n`argv` -> globals (`LOC`, `FILES`, risk flags, `JSON_OUTPUT`, `QUIET_OUTPUT`, `RECOMMEND_PHASES`) -> score functions -> `determine_level` -> `determine_phasing` (unless `--no-recommend-phases`) -> dispatch -> stdout. Quiet mode changes only the last step.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n## FIX ADDENDUM: AFFECTED SURFACES\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `output_text()` in `recommend-level.sh` | Produces the full human report | Update only to call the shared line helper; its surrounding output is unchanged | Byte-diff against a pre-change capture |\n| Output dispatch at the end of main | Chooses JSON or text | Update: add quiet as a third branch, with JSON first | `rg -n 'JSON_OUTPUT\\|QUIET_OUTPUT' recommend-level.sh` plus the new test |\n| `show_help()` and the header OPTIONS block | Document the interface | Update: add `--quiet` and an example to both | `recommend-level.sh --help \\| rg -n -- '--quiet'` |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml`, `speckit-complete.yaml` | Call the script with `--json` | Unchanged; JSON precedence keeps their output byte-identical | `rg -n 'recommend-level\\.sh' .skilled/commands` |\n| `runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js` | Asserts that JSON stdout holds the payload and nothing else | Unchanged; the JSON path is untouched | Focused vitest run |\n| `README.md`, `manual-testing-playbook/**` | Describe the script and its options | Unchanged; neither enumerates the option list | `rg -n 'recommend-level\\.sh' README.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook` |\n\nRequired inventories:\n- Same-class producers: `rg -n 'output_(text|json)|Recommended Level:' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` names every place that writes the report, so no third output path is missed.\n- Consumers of changed symbols: `rg -n 'recommend-level\\.sh' . --glob '!specs/**'` finds the command YAML, the README and the playbook pages; every programmatic consumer passes `--json`.\n- Matrix axes: output mode {text, quiet, json, json + quiet} x phase mode {default, `--no-recommend-phases`} x input {valid, invalid, missing}. The test covers one representative row per axis value rather than the full cross product.\n- Algorithm invariant: this is not a path, redaction or parser fix. The invariant is that non-quiet output is byte-identical and that the quiet line equals the first content line of the full report.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Unit | `output_quiet()` line format and the shared level helper | vitest, driving bash through `spawnSync` |\n| Integration | The full script across the output-mode matrix, plus the error paths | vitest |\n| Manual | Operator reads `--help` and runs one quiet invocation | terminal |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| Bash 3.2 (`/bin/bash` on macOS) | External platform | Green | A bash 4-only construct would break the default shell on the operator's machine |\n| vitest CLI project (`@spec-kit/cli`) | Internal | Green | The regression has no home and the change ships unverified |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: Default text output differs from the pre-change capture, or JSON output changes in any way.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single script change. The test file can be reverted with it. No data, schema or configuration is involved.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──────┐\n                      ├──► Phase 2 (Core) ──► Phase 3 (Verify) ──► Phase 4 (Docs)\nPhase 1.5 (Config) ───┘\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Core, Config |\n| Config | Setup | Core |\n| Core | Setup, Config | Verify |\n| Verify | Core | Docs |\n| Docs | Verify | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 15-30 minutes |\n| Core Implementation | Low | 45-60 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 30-45 minutes |\n| Documentation | Low | 15 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **2-2.5 hours** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [x] Backup created (if data changes) - not applicable; no data\n- [x] Feature flag configured - not applicable; the flag is the interface\n- [x] Monitoring alerts set - not applicable; a CLI script\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the `recommend-level.sh` change.\n2. Run the focused test to confirm the reverted script behaves as before.\n3. Re-run the full CLI suite.\n4. Notify nobody; the default interface never changed.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐\n│   Phase 1   │────►│   Phase 2   │────►│   Phase 3   │────►│   Phase 4   │\n│   Setup     │     │    Core     │     │   Verify    │     │    Docs     │\n└─────────────┘     └──────┬──────┘     └─────────────┘     └─────────────┘\n                          │\n                    ┌─────▼─────┐\n                    │  Phase 2b │\n                    │  Help text│\n                    └───────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline/*.txt` | Byte-diff verification |\n| Quiet output function | Shared level helper | One-line stdout | Dispatch branch |\n| Dispatch branch | Quiet output, `JSON_OUTPUT` | Correct mode selection | All output verification |\n| Regression test | Implementation | A failing-then-passing check | Completion |\n| Help text | None | Documented interface | Acceptance criterion AC-004 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture the pre-change output corpus** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Add the quiet output path and dispatch** - 30 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Byte-diff default output against the capture** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: about 1 hour\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The regression test can be written while the implementation is being written, because it only reads the script.\n- The help text and the header OPTIONS block can be edited after the behavior is settled, independently of the test.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Setup Complete | The baseline capture exists and the test harness runs | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Core Done | `--quiet` prints one line and default output is unchanged | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | Release Ready | The focused test and the full CLI suite pass; validation passes | Phase 4 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: Quiet mode is an output mode, and JSON takes precedence\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: The script already selects between two output modes with a boolean (`JSON_OUTPUT`). A second boolean creates a four-cell matrix, and two cells need an explicit answer: neither flag, and both flags. Programmatic callers in the speckit command assets pass `--json` and parse the document, and one test asserts that JSON stdout holds the payload and nothing else.\n\n**Decision**: `--quiet` is a text-output modifier. Dispatch order is JSON, then quiet, then text. `--json --quiet` prints the unchanged JSON document.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Callers that pass `--json` keep their exact contract, including the combination with `--quiet`.\n- `--quiet` is meaningful only for the human-readable path, which is what the request describes.\n- A caller that wants a bare number from JSON must extract `recommended_level` itself; that is the existing JSON contract and not this flag's job.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Quiet replaces JSON with the bare level number: breaks existing `--json` consumers and the test that asserts JSON purity.\n- Make `--json --quiet` an error: hostile to a caller that sets flags generically, and adds an exit path nobody needs.\n- Add a `-q` short alias now: the request names `--quiet`, and the alias is a separate interface decision.\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\nWritten for the implementer. Follow each rule as written; where a rule and your own judgment disagree, follow the rule and record the disagreement in `tasks.md`.\n\n### Roles\n- **Implementer**: edits `recommend-level.sh` and the new test file, then runs every verify command.\n- **Reviewer**: reads the diff against the acceptance criteria and writes nothing.\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n- [ ] Read `recommend-level.sh` in full before the first edit, including the header comment, `show_help()` and the dispatch block.\n- [ ] Read the existing `create-unknown-options.vitest.ts` for the harness pattern.\n- [ ] Capture the pre-change output corpus into `scratch/baseline/` before touching the script.\n- [ ] Confirm `bash --version` reports 3.2 or newer and `node --version` meets the project requirement.\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Requirement |\n|------|-------------|\n| TASK-SEQ | Run tasks in ID order; a task may start only after its predecessor's verify command passed. |\n| TASK-SCOPE | Edit only the two paths in the Files to Change table. |\n| TASK-VERIFY | Run the task's verify command and read both its output and its exit status before marking the task `[x]`. |\n| TASK-LOCATE | Find text by its exact content, never by a line number. |\n| TASK-UNTOUCHED | Do not reformat, reorder or reword any output line outside the level line. |\n| TASK-BANNED | Never run `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git switch`, `git branch`, `git reset`, `git stash`, `generate-context.js` or `/speckit:save`. |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\nAfter each task: `Task T### complete. Files: <paths>. Verify: <command> -> <observed result>.`\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\nIf a verify command fails twice for the same reason, stop, write the command and its full output to `scratch/blocked.md`, leave the task `[ ]`, and escalate. Do not mark a blocked task done and do not widen the scope to route around it.\n\n---\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"A quiet output mode for recommend-level.sh: one global, one parser case, one output function and one dispatch branch, with the level line shared by both human-readable paths so they cannot drift. JSON keeps precedence and default output stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2-compatible shell script |\n| **Framework** | None; `recommend-level.sh` is a standalone CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | vitest CLI project, driving the script through `spawnSync` |\n\n### Overview\nAdd a quiet output mode to `recommend-level.sh`. The change is a `QUIET_OUTPUT` global beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, one `--quiet` case in the existing argument loop, an `output_quiet()` function that prints the level line the full report starts with, and a dispatch branch that prefers JSON, then quiet, then text. Default output and every exit code stay exactly as they are.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-file CLI with one function per concern; the output format is a strategy sel