{"toolCallId":"call_6df76df76e7e45ca898d93b8966b1e90","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/222-deepseek-baseline/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n[2-3 sentence high-level overview for stakeholders who need quick context]\n\n**Key Decisions**: [Major decision 1], [Major decision 2]\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: [Blocking dependency]\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | [P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n[What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- [Deliverable 1]\n- [Deliverable 2]\n- [Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [Excluded item 1] - [why]\n- [Excluded item 2] - [why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [path/to/file.js] | [Modify/Create/Delete] | [Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: [Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [System/API] | [What if blocked] | [Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [Risk description] | [High/Med/Low] | [Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [Retry strategy]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [/25] | [Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [/25] | [Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [/20] | [Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [/15] | [Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [/15] | [Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[/100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [Risk] | [H/M/L] | [H/M/L] | [Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: [Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [user type], **I want** [needed behavior], **so that** [benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [user type], **I want** [needed behavior], **so that** [benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- [Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh answers one question but prints a sixteen-line report to answer it, so a caller that only needs the level slices stdout with head, grep or sed against a layout nobody promised. A --quiet flag that prints only the recommended-level line removes that slicing while leaving every existing invocation byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` decides which documentation level a task needs, then prints a sixteen-line report to say so. Any caller that only needs the level has to slice that report with `head`, `grep` or `sed`, and the slice is tied to a layout the script never promised. This packet adds `--quiet`, which prints only the recommended-level line and leaves every existing invocation byte-identical.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` is a text-output modifier rather than a fourth report; `--json` takes precedence when both flags are passed; the level line comes from one shared helper so the quiet and default paths cannot drift apart.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: Bash 3.2 compatibility, because macOS ships 3.2 as `/bin/bash`, and the existing vitest CLI project that holds the script's tests.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a blank line, the level, the score and confidence, a five-row breakdown and a phase-recommendation block, which is sixteen lines for `--loc 50 --files 2`. A caller that needs only the level must slice stdout with `head -1`, `grep '^Recommended Level:'` or `sed`, and every slice depends on a layout no interface promises. The script rejects `--quiet` today with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet` and exit 1, so slicing is the only option available.\n\n### Purpose\nOne flag, one line: `--quiet` prints only `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, so a caller reads the answer without parsing the report, while every invocation that does not pass the flag keeps its exact current output.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Accept `--quiet` in the option parser and record it in a `QUIET_OUTPUT` global.\n- Add a quiet output path that prints exactly the recommended-level line.\n- Document `--quiet` in the file header OPTIONS block and in `--help`, with an example.\n- Add a regression test covering the quiet line, unchanged default output, JSON precedence and the error paths.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Scoring, thresholds, confidence and phase logic - the flag changes presentation only, and those functions are not edited.\n- The JSON document shape - `--json` keeps its current output, including when `--quiet` is also passed.\n- A `-q` short alias - the request names `--quiet`, sibling scripts use `-q`, and adding one is a separate interface decision.\n- Callers and documentation outside the script - no caller needs a change because default output is unchanged.\n- Commit, push or branch operations - those stay with the operator.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | `QUIET_OUTPUT` global, `--quiet` parsing, quiet output function, dispatch branch, header and help text |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/recommend-level-quiet.vitest.ts` | Create | Regression test for the quiet line, unchanged default output, JSON precedence and error paths |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The parser accepts `--quiet` and sets quiet mode instead of reporting an unknown option. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet` and text output, stdout is exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, followed by one newline. There is no leading blank line and no score, breakdown or phase section. |\n| REQ-003 | Without `--quiet`, text output is byte-identical to the pre-change script for every existing invocation. |\n| REQ-004 | `--help` lists `--quiet` and includes an example, and the file header OPTIONS block lists the same option. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` combined with `--json` leaves the JSON document unchanged; `--json` takes precedence. |\n| REQ-006 | `--quiet` does not suppress stderr messages and does not change any exit code. |\n| REQ-007 | The regression test covers REQ-001 through REQ-006 in the existing vitest CLI project. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`, and the line reads `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)`.\n- **SC-002**: `recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2` produces output that diffs clean against a capture taken before the change.\n- **SC-003**: `recommend-level.sh --help` contains the `--quiet` option and an example that uses it.\n- **SC-004**: The focused test file and the full CLI vitest project both pass.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 (`/bin/bash` on macOS) | A bash 4-only construct would break the default shell on the operator's machine | Use 3.2-compatible syntax only; run `bash -n` and the suite on macOS |\n| Dependency | vitest CLI project | Without it the regression has no home and the change ships unverified | Add the test beside the existing CLI script tests |\n| Risk | The quiet and default paths drift apart | High: the two lines would disagree after a later edit | Both paths call one shared line-formatting helper |\n| Risk | `--json --quiet` is ambiguous | Medium: a caller could receive a different document than it parses today | Decide that JSON wins, state it in help, and test it |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: The quiet path adds no measurable work; one invocation completes well under 100 ms on the reference machine.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: The flag adds no input, file or network access, so the script's attack surface is unchanged.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Exit codes stay as they are - 0 for success, 1 for a usage or validation error - and `set -euo pipefail` stays in force.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--loc 0 --files 0` keeps its `At least --loc or --files must be provided` error and exit 1, with or without `--quiet`.\n- Maximum length: no new input is accepted, and numeric handling is untouched, so no new boundary exists.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- Missing or invalid value: `--quiet --loc` still prints `ERROR: --loc requires a numeric value` on stderr and exits 1.\n- Unknown option: unaffected; `--quiet` no longer reaches that branch.\n- `--help` with `--quiet`: help wins and exits 0 before any output mode runs.\n- `--json` with `--quiet`: JSON wins, and the document is byte-identical to a plain `--json` run.\n- Repeated `--quiet`: idempotent; the flag sets the same boolean twice.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 10/25 | Files: 2 (one script, one test), LOC: about 35, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 3/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N (default output unchanged) |\n| Research | 6/20 | Read the script, its callers and the CLI test harness |\n| Multi-Agent | 2/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 1 (the existing vitest CLI project) |\n| **Total** | **23/100** | **Level 1 by score; Level 3 by operator decision** |\n\nThe operator fixed the documentation level at 3 when scaffolding this packet (`create.sh --level 3`). The score records the change's own size and is not re-derived into a different level; the packet's verification depth follows the Level 3 contract.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | Default text output changes by accident | H | M | Byte-diff a pre-change capture before and after, and assert it in the test |\n| R-002 | The quiet line and the full report disagree | M | M | One shared helper formats the line for both paths |\n| R-003 | `--json --quiet` surprises an existing caller | M | L | Document JSON precedence in help and test the combination |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Just the level (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** command or agent that decides a documentation level, **I want** the script to print only its recommendation, **so that** I do not carry or parse a sixteen-line report to read one value.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Unchanged report (Priority: P0)\n\n**As an** operator reading the full report, **I want** today's output left untouched, **so that** existing habits, scripts and docs keep working.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Documented interface (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** maintainer of the spec-kit scripts, **I want** the flag in `--help` and in the file header, **so that** the option is discoverable without reading the parser.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- None outstanding. The `--json` precedence question is decided in REQ-005 and recorded as ADR-001 in `plan.md`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md`, ADR-001\n\n---\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":69,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":41}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/222-deepseek-baseline/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
